solution:
type HandlerMap<K extends string> = {
  [E in K as `on${Capitalize<E>}`]: E;
};

function capitalize<S extends string>(s: S): Capitalize<S> {
  return (s.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + s.slice(1)) as Capitalize<S>;
}

function handlerMap<const K extends string>(events: readonly K[]): HandlerMap<K> {
  const out: Record<string, string> = {};
  for (const event of events) out[`on${capitalize(event)}`] = event;
  return out as HandlerMap<K>;
}

`HandlerMap<K>` iterates the union of event names and uses an `as` clause to compute each key with the template literal type `on${Capitalize<E>}`, while the value stays the original literal `E`. The `const` modifier on `K` makes `['click', 'keyDown']` infer as the literal union `'click' | 'keyDown'` instead of `string`, so the mapped type has exact keys and `handlers.onclick` is an error. `capitalize` applies the same rule at runtime that `Capitalize` applies at compile time (upper-case the first UTF-16 unit, keep the rest), so the types and values agree; the only cast is at the boundary where a hand-built object becomes the mapped type. Duplicates overwrite the same key, the work is O(n) in the number of events, and an empty list gives `{}` with the type `{}`.

